    slug: "haryana-bagless-learning-programme",
    title: "Haryana introduces 10-day bagless learning programme in 250 PM SHRI schools",
    excerpt: "Haryana will implement a 10-day bagless learning initiative for practical exposure to vocational skills, crafts, and emerging career fields in PM SHRI schools.",
    category: "Education News",
    readTime: "4 min read",
    date: "Sep 14, 2026",
    author: "Growtopper Team",
    authorAvatar: "https://api.dicebear.com/9.x/notionists/svg?lips=&seed=Haryana&backgroundColor=e8d5f5",
    image: "/images/og-haryana-bagless.png",
    content: `<p>Haryana will introduce a <strong>10-day bagless learning programme</strong> in 250 PM SHRI schools during the 2026–27 academic session. The initiative will give students practical exposure to vocational skills, traditional occupations and emerging career fields beyond regular textbook-based classroom learning.</p><h2>What is the bagless learning programme?</h2><p>The programme is designed to make learning more practical and activity-based. During the designated days, students will participate in hands-on activities instead of following the usual classroom routine with school bags and regular textbooks.</p><p>The programme aims to help students understand how academic concepts are connected to real work, skills and careers.</p><h2>Who will participate?</h2><p>The programme will be implemented in <strong>250 PM SHRI schools across Haryana</strong> during the 2026–27 academic year.</p><p>Class 6 students will have a particularly important role. They will be required to prepare <strong>three projects during the academic year</strong>, based on the skills and activities covered during the bagless-learning days.</p><h2>What will students learn?</h2><p>The programme is expected to introduce students to:</p><ul><li>Vocational skills.</li><li>Traditional occupations.</li><li>Local crafts and professions.</li><li>Practical problem-solving.</li><li>Emerging fields and career options.</li><li>Basic workplace awareness.</li><li>Project planning and presentation.</li><li>Teamwork and communication.</li></ul><p>The exact activities may differ from one school to another depending on local resources, trainers and available facilities.</p><h2>Why is this programme important?</h2><h3>Learning beyond textbooks</h3><p>Many students understand a subject better when they can see or practise it in real life. Hands-on learning can make lessons more meaningful and easier to remember.</p><h3>Early career awareness</h3><p>Students often have limited knowledge of careers outside the most common options. Exposure to different skills can help them discover interests at an early age.</p><h3>Development of practical skills</h3><p>The programme can help students build teamwork, creativity, communication, decision-making and project-management skills.</p><h3>Connection with local opportunities</h3><p>Traditional occupations and local professions can show students how communities work and how skills contribute to the economy.</p><h3>Support for vocational education</h3><p>The initiative reflects the growing emphasis on combining academic learning with vocational and skill-based education.</p><h2>Class 6 project requirement</h2><p>Class 6 students will prepare three projects during the academic year. These projects will be connected to the skills and activities covered in the programme.</p><p>A project may involve:</p><ul><li>Researching a local occupation.</li><li>Documenting a traditional skill.</li><li>Creating a simple product or model.</li><li>Conducting an interview.</li><li>Presenting a local environmental or community problem.</li><li>Suggesting a practical solution.</li></ul><p>Students should follow the instructions given by their school because the project format, evaluation method and submission deadlines may vary.</p><h2>Budget and implementation</h2><p>The Haryana government has approved approximately <strong>₹9.3 crore</strong> for the programme. The funding will support hands-on learning activities across the participating PM SHRI schools.</p><p>Schools may use the funds for learning materials, training, workshops, field activities and project support.</p><h2>How students can benefit</h2><p>Students should actively participate instead of treating the programme as a holiday from normal studies. They can:</p><ul><li>Ask questions during practical sessions.</li><li>Maintain a learning notebook.</li><li>Record important observations.</li><li>Work responsibly in groups.</li><li>Connect activities with subjects such as Science, Mathematics and Social Science.</li><li>Begin collecting ideas for their Class 6 projects.</li><li>Practise explaining their work clearly.</li></ul><h2>What parents should know</h2><p>Parents can support the programme by:</p><ul><li>Encouraging children to participate.</li><li>Helping them complete projects independently.</li><li>Providing simple materials when required.</li><li>Respecting vocational and traditional skills.</li><li>Discussing what the child learned after each activity.</li><li>Avoiding excessive pressure about project marks.</li></ul><p>The purpose of the initiative is to build curiosity and practical understanding, not merely to produce decorative project files.</p><h2>Possible challenges</h2><p>The success of the programme may depend on:</p><ul><li>Availability of trained instructors.</li><li>Quality of school infrastructure.</li><li>Safety during practical activities.</li><li>Equal access across urban and rural schools.</li><li>Proper assessment of project work.</li><li>Coordination between schools and local professionals.</li></ul><p>Schools will need to ensure that activities are safe, inclusive and connected to genuine learning outcomes.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Haryana’s 10-day bagless learning programme is an important K–12 education initiative because it gives students opportunities to learn through activities, skills and real-world experiences. The programme will be implemented in 250 PM SHRI schools, with Class 6 students required to complete three related projects during the academic year.</p><p>If implemented effectively, the initiative can help students discover their interests, understand different careers and develop practical skills alongside textbook knowledge.</p>`
