    author: "Growtopper Team",
    authorAvatar: "https://api.dicebear.com/9.x/notionists/svg?lips=&seed=Global&backgroundColor=e8d5f5",
    image: "/images/og-brics.png",
    content: `<p>The <strong>BRICS New Delhi Declaration 2026</strong> has placed artificial intelligence, digital literacy and international student mobility at the centre of education cooperation among BRICS countries. The declaration supports the safe and human-centred use of AI in education and encourages countries to share effective teaching and learning practices.</p><h2>What is the BRICS education initiative?</h2><p>BRICS countries have agreed to strengthen cooperation in education, research and skills development. The initiative is intended to connect students, universities and researchers across member countries.</p><p>The declaration focuses on:</p><ul><li>Responsible use of AI in education.</li><li>Digital literacy.</li><li>Sharing of successful education practices.</li><li>Mutual recognition of qualifications.</li><li>Student and professional mobility.</li><li>University partnerships.</li><li>Scholarships and academic exchanges.</li><li>Early childhood education.</li><li>Foundational literacy and numeracy.</li></ul><h2>Proposed BRICS university ranking system</h2><p>The BRICS countries also backed further work on a proposed <strong>BRICS University Global Ranking and Evaluation System</strong>.</p><p>The proposed system could assess universities using criteria relevant to BRICS countries, such as:</p><ul><li>Quality of teaching.</li><li>Research output.</li><li>Innovation.</li><li>Industry cooperation.</li><li>Social impact.</li><li>International collaboration.</li><li>Contribution to local and national development.</li></ul><p>A specialised workshop on developing the ranking system is expected to take place between <strong>September and December 2026</strong>.</p><h2>Why is AI in education important?</h2><p>Artificial intelligence can support education in several ways:</p><ul><li>Personalised learning for students.</li><li>Faster feedback on assignments.</li><li>Translation and language support.</li><li>Digital tutoring.</li><li>Support for students with disabilities.</li><li>Data-based identification of learning gaps.</li><li>Improved teaching resources.</li></ul><p>However, BRICS leaders emphasised that AI should be used safely, ethically and in a human-centred manner. Technology should support teachers and students rather than replace human judgement completely.</p><h2>What does this mean for school students?</h2><p>Although many of the proposals focus on higher education, school students may also benefit from the wider cooperation.</p><p>Possible long-term effects include:</p><ul><li>Greater emphasis on digital literacy.</li><li>More AI-related learning opportunities.</li><li>International student competitions and exchanges.</li><li>Better access to online educational resources.</li><li>Stronger focus on STEM and innovation.</li><li>More scholarships and academic mobility opportunities.</li><li>Increased attention to safe and responsible use of technology.</li></ul><p>Students may also need to develop skills such as critical thinking, creativity, communication, collaboration and data awareness.</p><h2>Mutual recognition of qualifications</h2><p>BRICS countries have called for greater cooperation in the <strong>mutual recognition of qualifications</strong>.</p><p>If implemented, this could make it easier for students and professionals to study or work in another BRICS country. However, professional licensing rules may still apply separately for fields such as medicine, law and engineering.</p><p>The proposal is therefore a step towards easier academic mobility, but it does not mean that every degree will automatically be accepted for every profession in every country.</p><h2>Support for youth-led startups</h2><p>The declaration also supports cooperation in youth startups, research and innovation. BRICS countries plan to strengthen links between:</p><ul><li>Universities.</li><li>Research institutions.</li><li>Industries.</li><li>Student innovators.</li><li>Startup ecosystems.</li></ul><p>This could create more opportunities for students interested in entrepreneurship, technology and research-based innovation.</p><h2>How students can prepare</h2><p>Students can prepare for this changing education environment by:</p><ol><li>Building strong digital literacy.</li><li>Learning how to use AI tools responsibly.</li><li>Verifying information instead of accepting AI answers blindly.</li><li>Improving communication and teamwork skills.</li><li>Exploring coding, data science and STEM subjects.</li><li>Participating in innovation competitions.</li><li>Following scholarship and exchange opportunities.</li><li>Developing knowledge beyond the school textbook.</li></ol><p>Students should also understand important issues such as data privacy, plagiarism, misinformation and bias in AI systems.</p><h2>What is still uncertain?</h2><p>The declaration sets out areas of cooperation, but it does not immediately create:</p><ul><li>A common BRICS school syllabus.</li><li>Automatic acceptance of every qualification.</li><li>Guaranteed scholarships for all students.</li><li>A final university-ranking formula.</li><li>Immediate changes to CBSE or state-board examinations.</li></ul><p>Further agreements, frameworks and implementation plans will be required before students can directly use these opportunities.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>The BRICS New Delhi Declaration 2026 could influence the future of education by promoting responsible AI, digital literacy, international academic mobility and stronger university partnerships. For school students, the development highlights the importance of combining subject knowledge with technology, communication and problem-solving skills.</p><p>The proposed BRICS university ranking system, qualification recognition framework and youth-innovation initiatives could gradually create more international opportunities for students across member countries.</p>`
