    image: "/images/og-cbse-registration.png",
    content: `<p>The Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE) has asked affiliated schools to prepare for the registration of students in <strong>Classes 9 and 11</strong> and the submission of the <strong>List of Candidates (LOC)</strong> for students appearing in the <strong>Class 10 and Class 12 Board Examinations 2027</strong>. The process is expected to begin shortly through the official CBSE system.</p><h2>What is the CBSE notice about?</h2><p>CBSE has issued instructions to schools for the 2026–27 academic session. Schools must check and prepare student information before submitting it for:</p><ul><li>Class 9 registration.</li><li>Class 11 registration.</li><li>Class 10 LOC submission for the 2027 board examination.</li><li>Class 12 LOC submission for the 2027 board examination.</li></ul><p>The LOC is the official list through which schools inform CBSE which students will appear for the board examinations.</p><h2>Which students are affected?</h2><div class="overflow-x-auto my-6"><table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg"><thead class="bg-gray-50"><tr><th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-brand-dark uppercase tracking-wider">Student group</th><th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-brand-dark uppercase tracking-wider">What the school must do</th></tr></thead><tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-200"><tr><td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Class 9 students</td><td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500">Submit registration details</td></tr><tr><td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Class 11 students</td><td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500">Submit registration and subject details</td></tr><tr><td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Class 10 students</td><td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500">Include eligible candidates in the LOC for the 2027 board examination</td></tr><tr><td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Class 12 students</td><td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500">Include eligible candidates and selected subjects in the LOC for the 2027 board examination</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Students do not normally submit the LOC themselves. Their schools complete the process through the CBSE system.</p><h2>Details schools must verify</h2><p>CBSE has asked schools to keep the following information accurate and ready:</p><ul><li>Student’s full name according to the admission record.</li><li>Date of birth.</li><li>Gender.</li><li>Category, wherever applicable.</li><li>Full names of parents or guardians.</li><li>Subject choices and subject codes.</li><li>Recent photograph.</li><li>Student’s signature.</li><li>Parent or guardian details.</li><li>Other information required in the CBSE registration system.</li></ul><p>Schools should check every entry carefully before final submission.</p><h2>Why is this important for students?</h2><p>Errors in registration data can create problems later during:</p><ul><li>Admit-card generation.</li><li>Subject allocation.</li><li>Examination-centre preparation.</li><li>Mark-sheet and certificate production.</li><li>Result declaration.</li><li>Correction requests.</li></ul><p>For example, an incorrect subject code may result in a student being registered for the wrong subject. A spelling error in the name or date of birth can also create difficulties in official documents.</p><h2>What students and parents should do</h2><p>Students and parents should contact the school office or class teacher to verify:</p><ul><li>Name spelling.</li><li>Date of birth.</li><li>Parent or guardian names.</li><li>Selected subjects.</li><li>Photograph and signature.</li><li>Category and other personal details.</li><li>Whether the student’s registration has been successfully submitted.</li></ul><p>Any error should be reported to the school immediately, before the school locks or submits the final data.</p><h2>Important update for Class 10 students</h2><p>Under the revised examination arrangement, CBSE is expected to conduct <strong>two board examinations for Class 10 students</strong> and one board examination for Class 12 students. The exact schedule, eligibility rules and application procedure should be confirmed through an official CBSE notification.</p><p>The CBSE 2027 date sheet has not yet been released. Media reports expect the timetable later in 2026, but students should rely on the official CBSE website for the final schedule.</p><h2>Official CBSE information</h2><p>Schools and parents should check the official CBSE website:</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.cbse.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CBSE official website</a></li><li><a href="https://www.cbse.gov.in/cbsenew/list-of-circulars-related-to-student.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CBSE student-related circulars</a></li></ul><p>The CBSE student-circular page includes sections for registration, LOC, subject combinations, examination circulars and correction of particulars.</p><h2>Common mistakes schools must avoid</h2><p>CBSE has highlighted recurring problems such as:</p><ul><li>Incorrect student or parent details.</li><li>Wrong subject selection.</li><li>Incorrect subject codes.</li><li>Missing photographs or signatures.</li><li>Failure to submit the LOC.</li><li>Late submission.</li><li>Incomplete registration data.</li></ul><p>Schools should verify the information with students and parents before final submission.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>CBSE’s latest notice is an important early step in preparing for the 2027 board examinations. Students in Classes 9, 10, 11 and 12 should carefully verify their personal information and subjects with their schools.</p><p>The registration and LOC submission process will be handled by schools, but students and parents should actively check the submitted information to prevent errors in admit cards, marksheets and certificates. The official deadlines and application window should be followed only after CBSE publishes them on its website.</p>`
